  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cloudy, but very humid day has unfolded across northern New \nEngland. Thick cloud cover overhead should thin out through the \nafternoon, as low stratus progressively mixes out across the area. \nLess cloud cover later in the day will also allow for a marginal \nconvective environment to set up ahead of a very weak cold front. \nCAPE of 300-500J and low low-level wind shear should allow for \nslower-moving thunderstorms. A couple wind gusts can not be ruled \nout with some of the stronger storms that develop. Despite the \nstorms being slower-moving, storms will not move slowly enough to \ncause a flooding threat of any kind. The front is poised to stall \nover interior NH and ME tomorrow morning, allowing for showers to \ncontinue across the interior.\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms continue tomorrow afternoon as \nthe aforementioned front becomes quasi-stationary. Showers are \nstorms should still be pretty scattered, as low stratus and fog \novernight tonight should somewhat limit shower and storm development \non Tuesday. Showers become more isolated Tuesday night and more \npatchy fog could develop along the CT river valley and along the \ncoastal plain, likely lasting until sunrise Wednesday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front will approach New England Wednesday. While SW flow\nahead of it will bring warming temps into the lower 80s for the\nday, the front moves overhead with little fanfare as forcing \nweakens and moisture is inconsistent through the atmospheric \ncolumn. Sharpening of the trough looks to take place downstream,\nwith smoothing of the embedded wave that will pass through \nnorthern New England. Expect much of the precipitation to \ndissipate to the west, but some moisture could build on the \nwindward side of the Whites, resulting in isolated showers or \nsprinkles through the late afternoon and evening.\n\nIf the front continues to slow as the parent low occludes east\nof Hudson Bay, then it may wind up draped across the forecast\narea or just to the east. This would be the eventual pathway for\nlow pressure across the Ohio Valley to track into the latter\nhalf of the week. Thus precip chances Thursday and Friday could\neventually be in flux.\n\nHigh pressure should be nearing the region come the weekend, but\nthere will be the complicating factor of the slow moving \nboundary exiting, mentioned above, and a potential cut off low \nor trough arriving from the west. The combination of the low and\nreturn flow could spell for a damp period across much of the\nNortheast, but anticipate some slowing of the low's transit \neast which could help the first half of the weekend remain dry.",
